00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:18.100
Oh, wholesale change.

00:14:18.100 --> 00:14:20.700
Bobby Allison in car number 22 has taken the lead.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:23.040
Richard Petty has gone with him into second place.

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:25.440
Yarbrough suddenly finds himself in third,

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.600
and Earnhardt, who just seconds ago was dicing for the lead,

00:14:28.600 --> 00:14:30.000
is now back in fourth place.

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:32.970
- Try to imagine the force of the air

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:35.950
against a car when it is going 200 miles an hour.

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:37.610
That's the whole idea of this drafting.

00:14:37.610 --> 00:14:40.270
If you can be behind another car, as Petty,

00:14:40.270 --> 00:14:41.990
right now he's behind Allison,

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:44.930
and let that other car break that air out of the way for you,

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:47.770
you can run with as little as two-thirds throttle, Jim,

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:49.510
and still maintain your speed.

00:14:49.510 --> 00:14:52.050
- Of course, there's an element of trust involved in that.

00:14:52.050 --> 00:14:54.090
- Oh, tremendously.

00:14:54.090 --> 00:14:56.990
You have got to be sure that the men around you

00:14:56.990 --> 00:14:59.320
are not going to make a mistake, and that's why--

00:14:59.320 --> 00:15:00.000
- There goes Petty.

00:15:00.410 --> 00:15:02.070
And there goes Yarborough with him

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.530
as they go down the back straight.

00:15:03.530 --> 00:15:05.510
It is Richard Petty, now in turn three,

00:15:05.510 --> 00:15:06.690
who has taken the lead.

00:15:06.690 --> 00:15:09.330
They'll come out of turn four and onto the front straight now

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:10.470
