and started down low. And as they came

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:53.900
out, remember, this is the last turn before

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:55.980
the finish line. As they came out of the turn

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:57.900
with a lot of traffic ahead of them, it was

00:26:57.900 --> 00:26:59.890
really a drag race to the finish. They touched

00:27:00.030 --> 00:27:06.520
Briefly there, as they came toward the finish, it really was just a question of horsepower between the two and fate.

00:27:06.980 --> 00:27:09.700
And fate seemed to smile on Richard Petty this time.

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:12.340
Now watch the finish as they came down the front straight.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:13.760
There was the flag.

00:27:14.020 --> 00:27:15.080
There's the yellow line.

00:27:15.380 --> 00:27:17.720
Petty won by the nose of his car.

00:27:17.880 --> 00:27:23.980
They ran two more laps under the caution flag, but the race had been decided right there at that moment on your screen.

00:27:24.140 --> 00:27:27.610
When we come back, we'll be talking with Cale Yarborough, Richard Petty,

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:29.850
and the guest of honor, President Ronald Reagan.

00:27:31.630 --> 00:27:36.130
Back at the Daytona International Speedway here are the final standings as we suggested

00:27:36.130 --> 00:27:37.130
he might.

00:27:37.130 --> 00:27:41.770
Cale Yarborough sacrificed second place with that impromptu pit stop as Harry Gantt wound

00:27:41.770 --> 00:27:42.770
up second.

00:27:42.770 --> 00:27:48.670
But Richard Petty emerged victorious for his 200th Grand National Stock Car Racing victory.

00:27:48.670 --> 00:27:52.790
And right now, Larry Newber is in the garage with the disappointed Cale Yarborough.

00:27:52.790 --> 00:27:58.070
Cale, that is one eventuality you just can't predict, can you, a late yellow flag.

00:27:58.070 --> 00:28:00.000
No, you can't.

00:28:00.000 --> 00:28:04.380
right where I thought I wanted to be and just one of those things I got

00:28:04.380 --> 00:28:09.280
beat in my own game I guess. Of course, Richard's car was awful strong and then

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:16.040
I misread the flag with Spingers and when we came down for the caution flag

00:28:16.040 --> 00:28:21.110
I thought the race was over and I guess my brain blew up is what happened. It was

00:28:21.110 --> 00:28:25.140
just a dumb thing for me to do and I came in which lost some more positions.

00:28:25.140 --> 00:28:30.000
And now in an adjacent booth, Jim Lampley is with Richard Petty and the president.

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:34.340
and now here in what for today has become the winner's circle I am with the

00:28:34.340 --> 00:28:37.740
guest of honor President Ronald Reagan and the honored man of the day who has

00:28:37.740 --> 00:28:41.840
won his 200th Grand National Stock Car Race Richard Petty mr. president it's

00:28:41.840 --> 00:28:45.140
great to have you here a member of your staff intimated to me yesterday that

00:28:45.140 --> 00:28:48.140
although you are interested in the fortunes of all race drivers you might

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:52.040
be excited to see this occasion Richard Petty winning his 200 that's quite a day

00:28:52.040 --> 00:28:56.180
well it certainly is I understand that no one in the whole history of racing

00:28:56.180 --> 00:28:59.730
has ever done that or ever won 200 races

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:02.160
And you called four or five laps on the radio.

00:29:02.280 --> 00:29:03.400
How'd you think your race call went?

00:29:03.920 --> 00:29:04.900
Not very good.

00:29:05.000 --> 00:29:08.960
I'm glad that when I was a sports announcer, it was mainly baseball and football and a

00:29:08.960 --> 00:29:13.770
few things like swimming and that sort of thing, because I was having an awful lot of

00:29:13.770 --> 00:29:18.230
laps after I got here when I was trying to figure out who was where.

00:29:18.550 --> 00:29:22.710
I can tell you myself that it can be confusing, and it was a bit of a confusing finish.

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:26.880
I wonder, Richard, when you noticed that Heveron had left the track, the caution flag was going

00:29:26.880 --> 00:29:30.000
to come out and you had in effect a one-lap race to the flag for the

00:29:30.030 --> 00:29:34.130
the victory well just just to the one across the start finish line you know you're going in one

00:29:34.130 --> 00:29:37.170
direction then when you turn at the start finish line you look down into the first and second

00:29:37.170 --> 00:29:41.420
corner just as far as you can see and i've seen the car sitting on the the infield and when i did

00:29:41.420 --> 00:29:45.900
then i just put it to the floor and just waited for kale to make his move and when he did then

00:29:45.900 --> 00:29:49.420
he just went in the corner a little bit too hard up there and this car didn't stick and i was able

00:29:49.420 --> 00:29:53.100
to cut back the side of him and then we kind of touched a couple times and i beat him to the line

00:29:53.100 --> 00:29:57.000
you weren't worried when he went past you what you weren't worried when he went past you there

00:29:57.000 --> 00:29:58.010
there on the backstretch?

00:29:58.010 --> 00:29:59.010
Yeah, I was worried.

00:29:59.010 --> 00:30:00.000
I was sitting there

00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:02.880
what he was going I don't know what he was going to do and where he was going to do it but I didn't

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:08.130
know how I was going to overcome it but what happened when uh when that the boy spun then

00:30:08.130 --> 00:30:12.250
everything was was strictly reflex he had to do something I had to do something the figuring that

00:30:12.250 --> 00:30:18.520
both of us done never came to be so it was a act and a react he acted and I reacted you've been

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:23.180
around this sport so long that you may not get terribly excited at the prospect of winning a

00:30:23.180 --> 00:30:26.600
race but was there a little extra thrill in winning knowing that the number one fan was here

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:30.000
Well, definitely, you know, 200 is very, very important.

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:35.150
But under the circumstances, with all the presidents that's ever been in the United States,

00:30:35.230 --> 00:30:37.070
this is the first one that's ever showed up at a racetrack.

00:30:37.370 --> 00:30:40.210
So everybody's got to go from that, from a racing standpoint.

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:44.370
And I wanted to be the one that was able to welcome him to Grand National Racing.

00:30:44.660 --> 00:30:47.040
Richard, congratulations to you on this terrific occasion.

00:30:47.120 --> 00:30:48.380
Mr. President, thanks for being with us.

00:30:48.520 --> 00:30:49.780
Well, pleased to be here.

00:30:49.860 --> 00:30:51.380
And it's been a very exciting day.

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:53.540
And I join in the congratulations.

00:30:54.060 --> 00:30:55.960
I even have a conflict of interest here

00:30:55.960 --> 00:31:00.000
because he's doing some yeoman service

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:01.780
in a political sense.

00:31:03.980 --> 00:31:06.640
Well, the political aspirations of Richard Petty

00:31:06.640 --> 00:31:08.940
had nothing to do with this finish here.

00:31:08.940 --> 00:31:12.810
This was a matter of racing skill and horsepower

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:14.770
in one of the closest finishes ever.

00:31:14.770 --> 00:31:15.750
Look at that.

00:31:15.750 --> 00:31:17.290
Now, here is the final order of finish.

00:31:17.290 --> 00:31:18.630
Of course, the winner, Richard Petty.

00:31:18.630 --> 00:31:20.170
Harry Gantt got second place

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:22.190
as Cale Yarborough's brief tour of the pits

00:31:22.190 --> 00:31:23.100
dropped him to third.

00:31:23.100 --> 00:31:24.080
Bobby Allison, fourth.

00:31:24.080 --> 00:31:25.180
Benny Parsons, fifth.

00:31:25.180 --> 00:31:26.140
Bill Elliott, sixth.

00:31:26.140 --> 00:31:27.480
Terry Labonte, seventh.

00:31:27.480 --> 00:31:30.000
Dale Earnhardt, by finishing eighth, took over

00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:35.440
the top spot in the nascar point standings others david pearson the silver fox finished 17th

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:40.480
heberon who spun out to cause that exciting finish was treated and released at the track hospital

00:31:40.480 --> 00:31:45.600
as was the man who spun out earlier bobby hillen and daryl waltrip by finishing 31st

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:51.110
has dropped out of the points lead for nascar but once again the winner is 200th grand national

00:31:51.110 --> 00:31:55.970
victory the man they call king richard richard petty so that's the story from daytona beach

00:31:55.970 --> 00:31:57.390
And the Firecracker 400.
